= Algorithm engineering =

'''Algorithm engineering''' is a methodology for developing efficient algorithms through a repeated cycle of design, mathematical analysis, implementation, experimental evaluation, and redesign. It treats theoretical algorithmics and empirical performance measurement as complementary parts of one research process.

Peter Sanders describes algorithm engineering as a feedback cycle in which implementation and experiments reveal weaknesses in theoretical models and design assumptions, while analysis explains observed behavior and guides improved implementations.<ref name="sanders2009">[https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-03456-5_22 Sanders, P. (2009), “Algorithm Engineering – An Attempt at a Definition.”]</ref>

Important supporting elements include realistic machine models, algorithm libraries, representative benchmarks, reproducible implementations, and strong connection to applications.

== The engineering cycle ==

A typical cycle is:

<pre>
problem
→ model
→ design
→ analysis
→ implementation
→ experiment
→ interpretation
→ revised model or design
</pre>

The cycle can repeat many times.

A theoretical insight may improve implementation.

A benchmark result may expose a cache bottleneck not represented in the original model.

A production workload may reveal that the chosen benchmark distribution was unrealistic.

Algorithm engineering treats these discoveries as part of the research process.

== Problem specification ==

Before designing an algorithm, define the computational problem precisely.

Specify:

* valid inputs;
* required outputs;
* correctness conditions;
* constraints;
* objective;
* acceptable approximations.

An algorithm can be fast while solving the wrong problem.

Problem definition therefore precedes performance optimization.

== Computational models ==

Theory requires a model of computation.

Common models include:

* random-access machine;
* comparison model;
* external-memory model;
* parallel models;
* distributed models.

Each emphasizes different costs.

A RAM model may treat memory access as constant time.

Real hardware has:

* caches;
* memory latency;
* vector units;
* branch prediction.

Algorithm engineering asks when the theoretical model remains predictive and when a more realistic model is needed.

== Asymptotic analysis ==

Asymptotic complexity describes how resource use grows with input size.

For example, comparison sorting has familiar <math>O(n\log n)</math> algorithms.

Asymptotic notation suppresses:

* constant factors;
* cache behavior;
* instruction mix;
* implementation overhead.

Two algorithms with the same asymptotic complexity can differ greatly at practical scales.

Asymptotic analysis remains essential because measured speed at one size does not predict scaling automatically.

== Worst case and typical case ==

Worst-case analysis provides guarantees.

Typical workloads may behave differently.

Algorithm engineering often evaluates:

* worst-case inputs;
* random inputs;
* structured real-world inputs;
* adversarial inputs.

An algorithm optimized for average data can still need a fallback for pathological cases.

Benchmark design should reflect actual deployment risk.

== Implementation ==

Implementation translates an abstract algorithm into executable software.

Performance can depend on:

* memory layout;
* data structures;
* compiler;
* language;
* vectorization;
* concurrency;
* system calls.

An implementation is therefore part of the experimental object.

A slow implementation does not necessarily refute the algorithmic idea.

A highly optimized implementation can also obscure which design choice produced the gain.

== Constant factors ==

Suppose two algorithms take approximately

<math>T_A(n)=a n\log n</math>

and

<math>T_B(n)=b n\log n.</math>

If <math>a\ll b</math>, A can dominate across the relevant range even though the asymptotic class is identical.

Engineering aims to identify these constants and their causes.

== Crossover points ==

An algorithm with better asymptotic complexity can be slower for small inputs.

If

<math>T_A(n)=an^2</math>

and

<math>T_B(n)=bn\log n</math>

with <math>b\gg a</math>, there is a crossover size above which B becomes faster.

Hybrid algorithms exploit this.

Many production systems use one method for small subproblems and another for large ones.

== Memory hierarchy ==

Modern processors have multiple levels of memory.

Cache misses can dominate runtime.

An algorithm with more arithmetic but better locality can outperform one with fewer theoretical operations.

Cache-aware and cache-oblivious algorithms address this explicitly.

Measured performance should record hardware and memory configuration.

== Parallelism ==

Parallel algorithms add new costs:

* synchronization;
* communication;
* load imbalance;
* contention.

Speedup is

<math>S_p=T_1/T_p.</math>

Efficiency is

<math>E_p=S_p/p.</math>

Perfect linear speedup is rare.

A fair comparison reports total computational resources as well as wall-clock time.

== Input distributions ==

Algorithm behavior can depend strongly on input.

Sorting benchmarks might include:

* random permutations;
* nearly sorted arrays;
* reverse order;
* duplicate-heavy data;
* skewed keys;
* real records.

Testing only uniform random inputs can hide important weaknesses.

Input generators should be versioned and documented.

== Benchmark suites ==

A benchmark suite should represent the target problem family.

Good suites contain:

* multiple sizes;
* diverse structures;
* hard cases;
* real-world examples.

The benchmark should not be tuned after seeing results without disclosure.

Otherwise researchers can overfit the algorithm to the test set.

== Baselines ==

Performance claims require appropriate baselines.

Compare against:

* established algorithms;
* simple reference methods;
* current state of the art;
* ablated versions.

A new method outperforming an intentionally weak baseline provides little evidence.

Baseline implementations should receive comparable optimization effort.

== Correctness testing ==

Benchmark speed is meaningless if outputs are wrong.

Correctness checks can use:

* independent reference implementation;
* property-based testing;
* assertions;
* formal verification;
* known solutions.

For randomized algorithms, test many seeds.

For approximate algorithms, verify objective quality and constraints.

== Experimental reproducibility ==

A reproducible experiment records:

* source revision;
* build instructions;
* compiler version;
* flags;
* machine configuration;
* operating system;
* input data;
* random seeds;
* measurement commands.

This is part of [[Reproducible computational research]].

A performance number without environment metadata can be difficult to interpret.

== Measurement noise ==

Wall-clock timing varies because of:

* background processes;
* CPU frequency changes;
* thermal throttling;
* cache state;
* operating-system scheduling.

Experiments should use repeated runs.

Randomizing run order can reduce systematic drift.

Report distributions rather than only the fastest run.

== Statistical interpretation ==

For repeated measurements, useful summaries include:

* median;
* mean;
* quantiles;
* confidence intervals.

The best statistic depends on the noise distribution.

Tiny statistically detectable differences may be practically irrelevant.

Effect size matters.

== Tuning ==

Algorithms often contain parameters.

Tuning on the same benchmark used for final evaluation creates optimistic bias.

Better procedure:

<pre>
training/tuning instances
→ parameter choice
→ separate evaluation instances
</pre>

All compared methods should receive fair tuning budgets.

== Energy efficiency ==

Runtime is not the only engineering objective.

Metrics can include:

* energy;
* memory;
* communication;
* hardware cost.

Sanders explicitly notes advanced hardware and energy efficiency as algorithm-engineering concerns.<ref name="sanders2010">[https://doi.org/10.1137/1.9781611972900.6 Sanders, P. (2010), “Algorithm Engineering – An Attempt at a Definition Using Sorting as an Example.”]</ref>

The fastest algorithm may not be the most energy efficient.

== Robustness ==

An engineered algorithm should be tested outside ideal conditions.

Stress tests can vary:

* data size;
* skew;
* faults;
* resource limits;
* concurrency.

Robustness matters especially when algorithm performance influences critical systems.

== Libraries ==

Algorithm libraries preserve reusable implementations.

A library should expose:

* clear interfaces;
* complexity expectations;
* parameter defaults;
* tests;
* supported platforms.

Libraries also make experimental comparisons easier because strong implementations become accessible to other researchers.

== Application coupling ==

Algorithm engineering benefits from real applications.

Application constraints reveal which metrics matter.

For route planning, query latency may matter more than preprocessing time.

For batch analytics, throughput may dominate.

A theoretically elegant algorithm can be unsuitable if it optimizes the wrong resource.

== Profiling ==

Profilers identify where time is spent.

Possible bottlenecks include:

* memory;
* branch misprediction;
* synchronization;
* I/O.

Profiling prevents optimization based only on intuition.

It also helps separate algorithmic improvements from implementation artifacts.

== Algorithm portfolios ==

No one algorithm is best on every instance.

A portfolio chooses among several methods based on input features.

This is an engineering response to heterogeneous problem distributions.

Portfolio selection itself becomes an algorithmic problem.

== Reproducibility versus portability ==

Reproducing the same result on identical hardware is one goal.

Portable performance across architectures is another.

An algorithm tuned for one processor may degrade on another.

Evaluation should distinguish these claims.

== Algorithm engineering versus software engineering ==

Algorithm engineering focuses on efficient algorithmic methods and their empirical/theoretical validation.

Software engineering covers broader concerns such as:

* requirements;
* architecture;
* maintenance;
* testing;
* deployment.

A high-performance algorithm can still be embedded in poorly engineered software.

The fields overlap but are not identical.

== Epistemic boundary ==

Algorithm engineering does not replace proof with benchmarking.

Correctness and asymptotic analysis answer questions experiments cannot settle universally.

Experiments answer questions simplified models may miss.

The strongest results connect both.

